1037
00:46:41.230 --> 00:46:41.790
I do.

1038
00:46:42.030 --> 00:46:45.610
So many people wrote in questions for you about Pam.

1039
00:46:45.930 --> 00:46:48.730
And I just love our Office Ladies listeners.

1040
00:46:48.730 --> 00:46:50.790
They send in the best questions.

1041
00:46:50.790 --> 00:46:53.210
They're so thoughtful and wonderful observations.

1042
00:46:53.350 --> 00:46:55.370
We'll get to those after the break.

1043
00:46:56.030 --> 00:47:11.020
Back with our All About Pam Beasley episode.

1044
00:47:11.020 --> 00:47:13.800
And it is time for our fan Q&A.

1045
00:47:14.320 --> 00:47:16.300
I'm kicking us off with Raquel C.

1046
00:47:16.560 --> 00:47:18.320
from Michigan and Megan V.

1047
00:47:18.480 --> 00:47:19.200
from Columbia.

1048
00:47:19.720 --> 00:47:23.460
They wrote in and asked, where did the name Pam Beasley come from?

1049
00:47:23.600 --> 00:47:25.140
And then also Joe H.

1050
00:47:25.280 --> 00:47:28.780
from Long Island, New York says, were there ever any alternate first

1051
00:47:28.780 --> 00:47:30.080
and last names for Pam?

1052
00:47:30.500 --> 00:47:31.840
Well, here's what I can tell you.

1053
00:47:31.840 --> 00:47:37.640
All of my scripts, my breakdown, everything from the beginning of me being part of this

1054
00:47:37.640 --> 00:47:40.060
project, my name was Pam Beasley.

1055
00:47:40.540 --> 00:47:40.880
Mm-hmm.

1056
00:47:41.460 --> 00:47:42.400
Not Pamela.

1057
00:47:43.020 --> 00:47:43.260
Nope.

1058
00:47:43.680 --> 00:47:48.840
But I did reach out to Greg because he always has a good story for how he picked someone's

1059
00:47:48.840 --> 00:47:49.080
name.

1060
00:47:49.260 --> 00:47:49.460
Yeah.

1061
00:47:49.980 --> 00:47:54.040
First of all, he confirmed that this character's name has only ever been Pam Beasley.

1062
00:47:54.240 --> 00:47:55.740
There were no other alternates.

1063
00:47:55.900 --> 00:47:57.720
But here is how he came to name her.

1064
00:47:57.720 --> 00:48:04.220
Pam, to me, has a Midwestern sweetness that felt right, and I liked how similar the Jim

1065
00:48:04.220 --> 00:48:06.260
and Pam sounds are.

1066
00:48:06.480 --> 00:48:08.120
Oh, then they're both three letters.

1067
00:48:08.420 --> 00:48:08.600
Mm-hmm.

1068
00:48:09.440 --> 00:48:11.480
As for Beasley, that came later.

1069
00:48:11.880 --> 00:48:13.360
It has a humble feel.

1070
00:48:13.580 --> 00:48:17.820
Like generations ago, they were the village beekeeper who sold honey.

1071
00:48:17.820 --> 00:48:19.280
Oh, Greg.

1072
00:48:20.060 --> 00:48:21.280
I love him.

1073
00:48:21.980 --> 00:48:23.920
So that's how he came up with the name.

1074
00:48:24.120 --> 00:48:25.120
I love it.

1075
00:48:25.320 --> 00:48:25.400
Mm-hmm.

1076
00:48:26.600 --> 00:48:29.080
Next question is from Lucy P.

1077
00:48:29.320 --> 00:48:30.100
in Tennessee.

1078
00:48:30.780 --> 00:48:36.720
Lucy says, do you think Pam would have gone on a date with Toby if he ever asked her out

1079
00:48:36.720 --> 00:48:38.020
in season four?

1080
00:48:38.220 --> 00:48:40.100
Or would she have turned him down?

1081
00:48:41.800 --> 00:48:47.120
Um, Lucy, I don't think she would have gone on a date with him.

1082
00:48:47.260 --> 00:48:49.920
And when she said in that game, who would you do?

1083
00:48:50.080 --> 00:48:56.040
And she said, Oscar or Toby, she was just saying that because she couldn't say, Jim.

1084
00:48:56.600 --> 00:48:56.980
Right.

1085
00:48:57.520 --> 00:49:01.220
I don't think Pam ever saw Toby that way.

1086
00:49:02.100 --> 00:49:03.600
I don't think so either.

1087
00:49:03.800 --> 00:49:03.940
Yeah.

1088
00:49:04.200 --> 00:49:05.820
But I love that question.

1089
00:49:06.780 --> 00:49:08.680
Next up is Erin S.

1090
00:49:08.880 --> 00:49:09.640
from Maryland.

1091
00:49:10.120 --> 00:49:15.280
Erin says, one of the best things the writers on the show did was not to have Jim and Pam

1092
00:49:15.280 --> 00:49:17.300
get together right after season one.

1093
00:49:17.720 --> 00:49:21.900
It's very subtle when you're just watching through the episodes, but it's so powerful

1094
00:49:21.900 --> 00:49:26.620
that Pam was given the time to be on her own after ending her long engagement.

1095
00:49:27.300 --> 00:49:32.500
On the initial watch, it feels like Pam is being silly or cowardly, not just telling Jim

1096
00:49:32.500 --> 00:49:33.280
how she feels.

1097
00:49:33.280 --> 00:49:38.000
But I actually think that was the much more difficult and much braver decision.

1098
00:49:38.380 --> 00:49:43.320
Her personal growth during that period when Jim is away and then when he returns and dates

1099
00:49:43.320 --> 00:49:48.900
Karen is so great to watch and re-watching when the initial frustration of prolonging the

1100
00:49:48.900 --> 00:49:50.920
Jim and Pam's union is not so strong.

1101
00:49:51.460 --> 00:49:56.860
Can you share any thoughts you have about Pam's growth during this time and some of your favorite

1102
00:49:56.860 --> 00:49:59.000
storylines she had without Jim?

1103
00:49:59.000 --> 00:50:07.680
Oh, Erin, I absolutely loved season three as Pam for this very reason.

1104
00:50:08.000 --> 00:50:18.300
I loved season two, but I just love Pam figuring out who she is away from Roy and away from Jim.

1105
00:50:18.780 --> 00:50:19.920
I did too.

1106
00:50:20.040 --> 00:50:21.280
I thought it was really important.

1107
00:50:21.780 --> 00:50:22.480
Really important.

1108
00:50:22.480 --> 00:50:29.020
And in answer to your question about just some of my favorite storylines that I had without Jim,

1109
00:50:29.740 --> 00:50:35.100
because I think we do think of Jim and Pam together a lot, but there were great storylines

1110
00:50:35.100 --> 00:50:41.240
throughout my time in the office that I got to have that were all for Pam or that were Pam and other

1111
00:50:41.240 --> 00:50:44.900
people and I know, I know one of my favorites.

1112
00:50:44.900 --> 00:50:45.760
What is it?

1113
00:50:45.900 --> 00:50:47.180
Michael Scott Paper Company.

1114
00:50:47.360 --> 00:50:47.860
Yes.

1115
00:50:48.020 --> 00:50:53.700
I actually pulled a fan question that I saw from Audrey D in Lafayette, Louisiana.

1116
00:50:54.020 --> 00:50:54.940
My birth town.

1117
00:50:55.180 --> 00:50:55.600
Yes.

1118
00:50:55.800 --> 00:50:59.320
Who said, hi, ladies, I'm 15 and a huge fan.

1119
00:50:59.780 --> 00:51:05.800
I was wondering at what point in the show do you think Pam was at her happiest and most authentic?

1120
00:51:05.800 --> 00:51:09.740
For me, it seems like the Michael Scott Paper Company arc.

1121
00:51:10.080 --> 00:51:15.960
She feels more regulated, silly, happy, and genuine, even though she's technically farther from Jim.

1122
00:51:16.520 --> 00:51:18.400
Well, I have to agree with that too.

1123
00:51:18.400 --> 00:51:25.380
And I liked that we had these moments on the show where we got to see Pam flourishing separate from her love relationship.

1124
00:51:25.700 --> 00:51:26.140
Yeah.

1125
00:51:26.400 --> 00:51:30.140
And I made a list of a few others that I just loved getting to do on the show.

1126
00:51:30.520 --> 00:51:33.300
My nothing to do with Jim storylines.

1127
00:51:34.100 --> 00:51:34.620
Lice.

1128
00:51:35.540 --> 00:51:36.060
Lice.

1129
00:51:36.280 --> 00:51:38.800
I mean, I guess it kind of has something to do with Jim, but.

1130
00:51:38.940 --> 00:51:40.640
It's really about Pam and Meredith.

1131
00:51:40.760 --> 00:51:41.240
It is.

1132
00:51:41.380 --> 00:51:42.000
And I loved it.

1133
00:51:42.640 --> 00:51:46.660
Everything Party Planning Committee, especially the dueling Christmas parties.

1134
00:51:46.900 --> 00:51:47.180
Yes.

1135
00:51:47.400 --> 00:51:52.580
I loved her relationship with Karen in that episode, them teaming up.

1136
00:51:52.780 --> 00:51:52.940
Yeah.

1137
00:51:53.440 --> 00:51:58.040
I watched all of our Party Planning Committee scenes when I was researching Pam sass.

1138
00:51:58.540 --> 00:52:00.120
I wanted to play a bunch.

1139
00:52:01.120 --> 00:52:04.160
But I already felt like I had a lot of Pam sass.

1140
00:52:04.700 --> 00:52:06.680
But, oh, man, there's some good ones.

1141
00:52:07.340 --> 00:52:09.980
Well, I also loved Big Preggs, Little Preggs.

1142
00:52:10.100 --> 00:52:10.360
Yeah.

1143
00:52:10.800 --> 00:52:13.480
I love my cold open with the chore wheel.

1144
00:52:13.980 --> 00:52:14.480
Yes.

1145
00:52:14.560 --> 00:52:18.160
And Pam's just like love of order and chores and things.

1146
00:52:18.340 --> 00:52:19.100
That spoke to me.

1147
00:52:19.380 --> 00:52:19.600
Yeah.

1148
00:52:19.600 --> 00:52:26.960
And I loved the episode Vandalism when she teams up with Dwight and they, like, paint the truck and all that stuff.

1149
00:52:27.200 --> 00:52:28.260
I just love that.

1150
00:52:28.440 --> 00:52:36.160
And then I just, I loved every bit of her relationship to Michael, you know?

1151
00:52:36.320 --> 00:52:36.720
Yeah.

1152
00:52:36.720 --> 00:52:39.720
And that was very separate from her relationship with Jim.

1153
00:52:40.360 --> 00:52:48.100
It's so fun in doing these All About episodes because I feel like when I watched the show, I watched it for the whole ensemble.

1154
00:52:48.380 --> 00:52:53.840
But in doing these All About, I go specifically to these scenes for this one character.

1155
00:52:54.060 --> 00:52:54.280
Yeah.

1156
00:52:54.280 --> 00:53:00.860
And I watched a lot of the Michael Scott Paper Company episodes and just Pam and Michael on the road together.

1157
00:53:01.140 --> 00:53:01.480
Yeah.

1158
00:53:01.680 --> 00:53:03.060
It's so good.

1159
00:53:03.260 --> 00:53:05.060
It was so fun to do.

1160
00:53:06.000 --> 00:53:08.760
Next question is from Matt V in Delaware.

1161
00:53:08.760 --> 00:53:21.120
Matt says, while I truly love every aspect of the office, the singular thing that consistently still blows me away is every time the way Pam looks at Jim compared to anything else.

1162
00:53:21.300 --> 00:53:24.920
There is something just so incredibly natural and real about it.

1163
00:53:25.200 --> 00:53:33.080
He goes on to say, Jenna, how did you tap into something that felt so genuinely real for these non-speaking portions of the role?

1164
00:53:33.280 --> 00:53:37.080
Was there a crush from your childhood you were pulling back up the feelings from?

1165
00:53:37.080 --> 00:53:41.700
Or was it just through trying to truly feel what you believe Pam was feeling?

1166
00:53:42.600 --> 00:53:46.980
Well, Matt, I appreciate this question because a lot of the role of Pam Beasley is nonverbal.

1167
00:53:47.420 --> 00:53:49.280
You know, it's a lot of reacting.

1168
00:53:49.640 --> 00:53:49.900
Yes.

1169
00:53:50.080 --> 00:53:53.520
And so I had to really say a lot without a lot of dialogue.

1170
00:53:54.180 --> 00:53:57.900
So I went back to a couple of exercises that I did in acting school.

1171
00:53:58.600 --> 00:54:01.660
One of them was called Private Moment.

1172
00:54:01.980 --> 00:54:05.240
And it's an exercise in stillness.

1173
00:54:05.240 --> 00:54:15.020
You have to come to class and recreate an ordinary moment and try to do it in front of people as unselfconsciously as possible.

1174
00:54:15.780 --> 00:54:22.300
It's supposed to be a moment in silence without any speaking and also something that you would normally do alone.

1175
00:54:22.300 --> 00:54:25.660
But it should be very ordinary and very simple.

1176
00:54:25.680 --> 00:54:34.980
So it could be as simple as putting on shoes in the morning or brushing your teeth or washing your face or sitting on your bed and writing in a journal.

1177
00:54:35.500 --> 00:54:41.620
And you just have to move through that moment without trying to push at all.

1178
00:54:41.840 --> 00:54:45.180
And without even thinking that anyone is watching you.

1179
00:54:45.360 --> 00:54:48.560
It's the private, quiet time that when you're alone in your home.

1180
00:54:48.560 --> 00:54:51.840
Yes, completely non-performative in any way.

1181
00:54:52.640 --> 00:54:56.840
We also had to do these acting exercises called interior monologues.

1182
00:54:56.940 --> 00:55:04.520
And this is where in your script you go through and you write everything your character is thinking but not saying.

1183
00:55:04.840 --> 00:55:05.060
Yeah.

1184
00:55:05.060 --> 00:55:10.520
And when I played Pam, I was always asking myself, what is Pam not saying?

1185
00:55:11.480 --> 00:55:13.900
And, you know, we do this in our life all the time.

1186
00:55:13.900 --> 00:55:19.780
We can sit in a work meeting and we're literally talking but also making a grocery list in our head.

1187
00:55:19.920 --> 00:55:22.660
You know, it's like we can think one thing and say another thing.

1188
00:55:22.660 --> 00:55:25.140
So that was another thing I brought to Pam.

1189
00:55:25.360 --> 00:55:34.500
And early on I would write her interior monologues in my script and I would actively think them as if they were like alternate lines for my brain.

1190
00:55:34.860 --> 00:55:39.900
And that's how I tried to get certain things to play out on my face when I didn't have dialogue.

1191
00:55:39.900 --> 00:55:49.240
So, like, if there was a moment in the script where I was supposed to steal a glance at Jim, I would think to myself, don't look at Jim, don't look at Jim, don't look at Jim.

1192
00:55:49.380 --> 00:55:50.540
And then I would look at him.

1193
00:55:50.820 --> 00:55:55.720
And I would sort of hope that that would reveal how much Pam really wanted to look at him.

1194
00:55:55.820 --> 00:55:56.100
Yeah.

1195
00:55:56.400 --> 00:55:58.760
Because I would be thinking, don't look at him, don't look at him.

1196
00:55:58.820 --> 00:55:58.960
Yeah.

1197
00:55:59.260 --> 00:55:59.880
Don't look at him.

1198
00:56:00.940 --> 00:56:01.860
That's so good.

1199
00:56:02.540 --> 00:56:08.320
I would write, like, one word next to, like, a paragraph if I had dialogue,

1200
00:56:08.320 --> 00:56:17.720
whatever it might be that I thought Angela was feeling down deep about something because she often was just so kind of hostile and abrupt.

1201
00:56:17.940 --> 00:56:22.660
But maybe that day she was feeling fearful or sad or anxious.

1202
00:56:22.660 --> 00:56:28.340
Like, so I would just, like, write an emotion word to go along with what was being said.

1203
00:56:28.340 --> 00:56:36.820
Which is why I think your character was so layered rather than, like, that one note of bitchiness.

1204
00:56:37.040 --> 00:56:41.000
You always had your secondary emotion that you were playing.

1205
00:56:41.260 --> 00:56:42.340
I definitely tried.

1206
00:56:42.640 --> 00:56:42.960
Yes.

1207
00:56:43.340 --> 00:56:44.560
That's good acting.

1208
00:56:44.900 --> 00:56:49.740
Well, I really saw so much emotion in Pam and just your eyes, Jenna.

1209
00:56:49.980 --> 00:56:54.200
I thought you were just so good at conveying so much.

1210
00:56:54.620 --> 00:56:56.300
Really good acting class.

1211
00:56:56.480 --> 00:56:57.880
Watch Jenna as Pam, guys.

1212
00:56:58.340 --> 00:56:58.700
Aw.

1213
00:56:58.960 --> 00:56:59.540
Thanks, lady.

1214
00:56:59.820 --> 00:57:00.180
Mm-hmm.

1215
00:57:01.300 --> 00:57:02.020
Allison W.

1216
00:57:02.360 --> 00:57:04.040
from Winnipeg wrote in to say,

1217
00:57:04.220 --> 00:57:09.300
Jenna, when did you realize your character was a part of one of television's greatest love stories?

1218
00:57:09.540 --> 00:57:11.940
How did that feel then and now?

1219
00:57:12.260 --> 00:57:13.240
And Leighton R.

1220
00:57:13.520 --> 00:57:15.400
wrote in from Folsom, California, saying,

1221
00:57:15.900 --> 00:57:18.360
Pam's relationship with Jim is a fan favorite.

1222
00:57:18.640 --> 00:57:24.040
How did you and John Krasinski develop the chemistry that made their bond so believable?

1223
00:57:25.640 --> 00:57:36.580
I have to tell you, I just saw a trailer for Will Ferrell's new movie with Reese Witherspoon and literally their daughter is at her wedding day doing her vows and she says,

1224
00:57:36.580 --> 00:57:37.840
you're the Jim to my Pam.

1225
00:57:38.040 --> 00:57:38.420
What?

1226
00:57:38.740 --> 00:57:40.640
And this movie that is just coming out.

1227
00:57:40.880 --> 00:57:42.040
Oh, my gosh.

1228
00:57:42.300 --> 00:57:43.280
Isn't that wild?

1229
00:57:43.380 --> 00:57:44.220
That is nuts.

1230
00:57:44.760 --> 00:57:45.020
Yeah.

1231
00:57:45.400 --> 00:57:54.080
That is, I mean, just one example of how ingrained, I feel like, into pop culture are Jim and Pam.

1232
00:57:54.620 --> 00:57:54.860
Yes.

1233
00:57:55.320 --> 00:57:55.620
Yes.

1234
00:57:55.620 --> 00:58:04.200
And I saw this question as well, Angela, and I'm so excited to get to talk about this because people ask all the time about my chemistry with John.

1235
00:58:04.540 --> 00:58:12.160
And for a long time when the show was on the air and even afterward, people would get upset when they learned we were not a couple in real life.

1236
00:58:12.340 --> 00:58:12.820
I know.

1237
00:58:12.920 --> 00:58:14.140
People so want to believe it.

1238
00:58:14.520 --> 00:58:18.880
And I did this interview once where I talked about all this in a really clunky way.

1239
00:58:18.880 --> 00:58:23.740
And I was then misquoted and all these clickbait articles went viral.

1240
00:58:24.120 --> 00:58:29.240
And the misquote has gone on to be reprinted over and over again.

1241
00:58:29.240 --> 00:58:31.280
I even saw it in a book, Angela.

1242
00:58:32.140 --> 00:58:33.820
I mean, I remember this.

1243
00:58:34.740 --> 00:58:36.000
It was just crazy.

1244
00:58:36.460 --> 00:58:37.820
Do you want to share the misquote?

1245
00:58:38.260 --> 00:58:38.720
Yes.

1246
00:58:38.940 --> 00:58:40.360
Since we're talking about this.

1247
00:58:40.460 --> 00:58:41.960
I mean, this is the time.

1248
00:58:42.180 --> 00:58:46.700
I was on Watch What Happens Live, which, by the way, is a show that I love.

1249
00:58:46.800 --> 00:58:48.440
And Andy Cohen is amazing.

1250
00:58:48.440 --> 00:58:49.440
Such a fun show.

1251
00:58:49.560 --> 00:58:50.540
Also from St. Louis.

1252
00:58:51.500 --> 00:58:53.960
So this is no reflection on it.

1253
00:58:54.360 --> 00:59:00.660
But a fan called in to ask how John and I had such great chemistry while not being a couple in real life.

1254
00:59:01.060 --> 00:59:02.220
And I said this quote.

1255
00:59:02.340 --> 00:59:03.260
I looked it up, Ange.

1256
00:59:03.380 --> 00:59:04.440
Here is my quote.

1257
00:59:04.620 --> 00:59:05.240
Direct quote.

1258
00:59:05.440 --> 00:59:05.620
Yes.

1259
00:59:05.700 --> 00:59:06.560
I said this.

1260
00:59:07.780 --> 00:59:13.360
Quote, there's like a real part of me that is Pam and a real part of him that's Jim.

1261
00:59:13.780 --> 00:59:17.460
And those parts of us were genuinely in love with one another.

1262
00:59:18.440 --> 00:59:22.340
In real life, we aren't totally Pam and totally Jim.

1263
00:59:22.580 --> 00:59:26.380
So in real life, we're not like the perfect match.

1264
00:59:26.880 --> 00:59:30.180
He was like a type of spouse that I had for a long time.

1265
00:59:30.180 --> 00:59:31.380
He was my partner.

1266
00:59:31.380 --> 00:59:33.800
And we will always be close because of it.

1267
00:59:33.920 --> 00:59:37.530
That makes perfect sense to me.

1268
00:59:37.630 --> 00:59:39.690
It's a very actory answer.

1269
00:59:39.950 --> 00:59:40.950
It's a little actory.

1270
00:59:41.050 --> 00:59:42.010
It's a little clunky.

1271
00:59:42.490 --> 00:59:45.050
Well, I mean, I get it, though.

1272
00:59:45.210 --> 00:59:46.710
You're I hear what you're saying.

1273
00:59:46.870 --> 00:59:54.190
You're saying that as an actor, the part of you that was Pam and as an actor, the part of him that's Jim had a real bond.

1274
00:59:54.190 --> 00:59:56.910
But you say it's not in real life.

1275
00:59:57.090 --> 00:59:57.950
Not in real life.

1276
00:59:58.110 --> 01:00:00.430
Oh, but now people misquoted it.

1277
01:00:00.710 --> 01:00:04.330
Well, is it misquoting or is it click baiting?

1278
01:00:05.050 --> 01:00:05.530
Because.

1279
01:00:05.810 --> 01:00:06.750
Yeah, it's click baiting.

1280
01:00:06.790 --> 01:00:08.270
I feel like it was click baity.

1281
01:00:08.730 --> 01:00:11.610
I mean, I know why it was hard to describe it.

1282
01:00:11.710 --> 01:00:13.230
It's because you don't want to let people down.

1283
01:00:13.410 --> 01:00:13.610
Right.

1284
01:00:13.610 --> 01:00:21.910
Yes, I was always afraid to tell people that in real life we're just friends, you know, because the fandom for Jim and Pam is so strong.

1285
01:00:22.130 --> 01:00:28.030
But, yeah, we were just great scene partners and we had this deep mutual respect for one another.

1286
01:00:28.450 --> 01:00:29.130
That's it.

1287
01:00:29.250 --> 01:00:30.430
We never dated or anything.

1288
01:00:30.650 --> 01:00:31.210
Just friends.

1289
01:00:31.750 --> 01:00:34.710
But, oh, my goodness, Angela, the click bait.

1290
01:00:34.970 --> 01:00:35.550
I know.

1291
01:00:35.750 --> 01:00:39.070
I woke up to my phone blowing up.

1292
01:00:39.210 --> 01:00:40.570
And it was not just tabloids.

1293
01:00:40.730 --> 01:00:41.930
This was everywhere.

1294
01:00:41.930 --> 01:00:45.470
It was like every website and on TV shows.

1295
01:00:45.670 --> 01:00:46.190
It was insane.

1296
01:00:46.650 --> 01:00:50.750
Well, it was like people picked it up like it was news, which is bonkers.

1297
01:00:50.990 --> 01:00:52.650
But I looked up some of the headlines.

1298
01:00:52.810 --> 01:00:53.310
Do you want to hear them?

1299
01:00:53.550 --> 01:00:53.730
Yes.

1300
01:00:53.850 --> 01:00:55.150
Is it OK if I read them?

1301
01:00:55.270 --> 01:00:59.670
Yes, because I read the quote and now look at what all the headlines said.

1302
01:01:00.210 --> 01:01:00.410
OK.

1303
01:01:01.650 --> 01:01:02.050
L.A.

1304
01:01:02.050 --> 01:01:02.650
Times.

1305
01:01:03.030 --> 01:01:04.910
The Los Angeles Times.

1306
01:01:05.610 --> 01:01:11.310
Quote, Jenna Fisher was genuinely in love with Office co-star John Krasinski.

1307
01:01:11.310 --> 01:01:12.390
I never said that.

1308
01:01:12.510 --> 01:01:14.130
You never said that.

1309
01:01:14.970 --> 01:01:16.090
Entertainment Weekly.

1310
01:01:16.330 --> 01:01:22.650
Jenna Fisher reveals she and John Krasinski were genuinely in love while filming The Office.

1311
01:01:23.110 --> 01:01:26.950
Boy, they really latched on to that genuinely in love thing.

1312
01:01:27.130 --> 01:01:31.070
Why are you allowed to take three words and turn it into a new context?

1313
01:01:31.070 --> 01:01:31.610
I don't know.

1314
01:01:31.610 --> 01:01:32.610
Is what I don't understand.

1315
01:01:32.890 --> 01:01:32.940
I don't know.

1316
01:01:33.870 --> 01:01:40.490
BuzzFeed wrote, Jenna Fisher and John Krasinski were genuinely in love while filming The Office and now our hearts are melting.

1317
01:01:40.970 --> 01:01:41.410
Yes.

1318
01:01:41.830 --> 01:01:41.970
Yes.

1319
01:01:41.970 --> 01:01:43.670
Now we're adding emotion to it.

1320
01:01:43.910 --> 01:01:44.430
Well, yes.

1321
01:01:44.430 --> 01:01:46.030
And it goes on and on like that.

1322
01:01:46.330 --> 01:01:52.470
All the articles just pulled the phrase genuinely in love as if the context of what I said wasn't important.

1323
01:01:52.790 --> 01:01:55.690
I never said we were genuinely in love.

1324
01:01:55.690 --> 01:01:56.170
Yeah.

1325
01:01:56.430 --> 01:01:57.710
So here's the thing.

1326
01:01:57.830 --> 01:02:02.130
When something like that happens to you and your phone's blowing up, what do you do?

1327
01:02:02.450 --> 01:02:03.130
I know.

1328
01:02:03.770 --> 01:02:04.530
I know.

1329
01:02:04.630 --> 01:02:05.310
What do you do?

1330
01:02:05.570 --> 01:02:14.190
Now it's like it's so crazy because no matter how much you like try to be like correct it, it is out in the world.

1331
01:02:14.410 --> 01:02:14.530
Yeah.

1332
01:02:14.790 --> 01:02:15.810
Well, here's what I did.

1333
01:02:15.970 --> 01:02:16.650
I called John.

1334
01:02:17.130 --> 01:02:20.450
He answers the phone and he goes, hey.

1335
01:02:20.970 --> 01:02:21.530
Oh, no.

1336
01:02:21.750 --> 01:02:23.030
He gave you a Stacey.

1337
01:02:23.910 --> 01:02:25.250
He had seen it all.

1338
01:02:25.250 --> 01:02:27.250
Oh, his phone had been blowing up, too.

1339
01:02:27.510 --> 01:02:30.990
And I was like, John, I was click baited.

1340
01:02:31.090 --> 01:02:32.790
And he said, oh, I know.

1341
01:02:32.930 --> 01:02:34.210
I read the whole quote.

1342
01:02:34.390 --> 01:02:39.590
And you guys, you have to know that me and Lee are good friends with John and Emily.

1343
01:02:39.870 --> 01:02:40.130
Yeah.

1344
01:02:40.210 --> 01:02:41.630
You guys have done vacations together.

1345
01:02:41.790 --> 01:02:43.550
You've done playdates with your kids.

1346
01:02:43.750 --> 01:02:43.970
Yes.

1347
01:02:44.350 --> 01:02:48.190
And John says, you know, I actually have a press junket coming up.

1348
01:02:48.350 --> 01:02:50.850
So I'm happy to say that you were misquoted.

1349
01:02:51.070 --> 01:02:51.430
I know.

1350
01:02:51.630 --> 01:02:53.310
I was like, John, no.

1351
01:02:53.310 --> 01:02:56.410
Oh, everyone's going to ask you about this now at this press junket.

1352
01:02:56.550 --> 01:02:57.770
He said, don't worry about it.

1353
01:02:57.850 --> 01:02:59.050
I'll say you were misquoted.

1354
01:02:59.290 --> 01:03:00.650
And I said, that's great.

1355
01:03:00.890 --> 01:03:01.650
Just simple.

1356
01:03:01.850 --> 01:03:02.910
She was misquoted, guys.

1357
01:03:03.710 --> 01:03:03.890
OK.

1358
01:03:03.970 --> 01:03:05.130
Well, that did not go well either.

1359
01:03:05.130 --> 01:03:14.530
After he tries to set the record straight, all these headlines come out that say, John Krasinski says he was not in love with Jenna Fisher.

1360
01:03:14.750 --> 01:03:19.090
But all the stories still imply that I was in love with him.

1361
01:03:19.090 --> 01:03:21.810
So that news cycle goes out.

1362
01:03:22.050 --> 01:03:23.350
That's not helping.

1363
01:03:23.610 --> 01:03:23.870
No.

1364
01:03:24.110 --> 01:03:26.630
And so now my phone is blowing up again.

1365
01:03:26.630 --> 01:03:28.470
And John calls me.

1366
01:03:28.650 --> 01:03:30.130
And I'm like, hey.

1367
01:03:31.510 --> 01:03:34.150
And he's like, I am so sorry.

1368
01:03:34.910 --> 01:03:36.510
I was trying to help.

1369
01:03:36.790 --> 01:03:39.670
And at that point, I said, just forget it.

1370
01:03:39.970 --> 01:03:40.990
Just forget it.

1371
01:03:41.070 --> 01:03:42.470
I'm never getting rid of this.

1372
01:03:42.670 --> 01:03:45.350
And of course, here I am talking about it again.

1373
01:03:45.350 --> 01:03:49.810
And I guess probably opening myself up to more of this clickbait bullshit.

1374
01:03:49.810 --> 01:03:57.790
I guess my long answer to the fan question, when did I realize that I was part of one of television's greatest love stories?

1375
01:03:58.010 --> 01:04:00.130
And how did that feel then and now?

1376
01:04:00.410 --> 01:04:05.590
Well, I realized pretty early on that people have a strong attachment to Jim and Pam.

1377
01:04:05.970 --> 01:04:14.470
But I never really thought about how that attachment might become sort of weird with like.

1378
01:04:14.650 --> 01:04:15.470
Global news story.

1379
01:04:15.930 --> 01:04:17.710
Clickbait articles and stuff.

1380
01:04:19.030 --> 01:04:20.770
Yeah, I mean, that is weird.

1381
01:04:20.770 --> 01:04:24.490
And it's kind of a drag because, I mean, love Jim and Pam.

1382
01:04:24.730 --> 01:04:25.430
Love them.

1383
01:04:25.590 --> 01:04:27.870
But this part of it is a little bit of a bummer.

1384
01:04:28.270 --> 01:04:30.210
And, you know, I never had that with Rain.

1385
01:04:31.330 --> 01:04:34.630
I don't think Dwangela quite packs the punch like jam.

1386
01:04:35.650 --> 01:04:44.090
Well, you know, I remember early in my career for a really long time, there was this misquote about me that was in an article I was doing about animal rescue.

1387
01:04:44.090 --> 01:04:53.770
And I had said in my interview that during my time in animal rescue, I said that I had rescued and rehabilitated and found homes for 12 cats.

1388
01:04:53.970 --> 01:04:58.010
Well, the article just said I had 12 cats.

1389
01:04:58.730 --> 01:05:05.710
So like for years when I would do other interviews, they would ask me about my 12 cats.

1390
01:05:05.850 --> 01:05:08.170
And I would have to say, I don't have 12 cats.

1391
01:05:08.270 --> 01:05:13.210
So weird the things that somehow get distorted and people latch on to.

1392
01:05:13.710 --> 01:05:16.930
This is nowhere, nowhere like what you've been through.

1393
01:05:17.090 --> 01:05:20.290
But one time in an interview, someone asked me what my favorite food was.

1394
01:05:20.290 --> 01:05:21.830
And I said, I love tacos.

1395
01:05:23.010 --> 01:05:25.350
I don't know you to love tacos.

1396
01:05:25.610 --> 01:05:26.150
I don't know.

1397
01:05:26.230 --> 01:05:27.130
I was on the spot.

1398
01:05:27.310 --> 01:05:29.950
I was like, it was like on a red carpet, you know, like, what's your favorite food?

1399
01:05:30.090 --> 01:05:31.370
And I was like, I don't know, tacos.

1400
01:05:32.430 --> 01:05:35.850
I mean, I can't tell you how many times people ask me like, hey.

1401
01:05:35.850 --> 01:05:39.310
Hey, we know you love tacos.

1402
01:05:39.710 --> 01:05:46.010
Do you ever arrive places and they've put tacos like in your dressing room because they Googled you and they know how much you love them?

1403
01:05:46.790 --> 01:05:50.770
No, but I did have a gift basket one time that had taco seasoning in it.

1404
01:05:51.110 --> 01:05:52.430
That's what I'm talking about.

1405
01:05:52.650 --> 01:05:53.570
That's what I'm saying.

1406
01:05:54.370 --> 01:05:57.730
Oh, well, I think it's on my Wikipedia page.

1407
01:05:58.010 --> 01:05:58.810
It might be.

1408
01:05:59.050 --> 01:06:02.050
Your love of tacos and my 12 cats.

1409
01:06:02.050 --> 01:06:03.430
I said it one time.

1410
01:06:03.650 --> 01:06:05.030
And my genuinely in love.

1411
01:06:05.030 --> 01:06:06.110
Oh, lady.

1412
01:06:06.450 --> 01:06:14.290
Well, listen, the bottom line is I am really proud of the work that John and I did on the show.

1413
01:06:14.530 --> 01:06:15.250
Really proud.

1414
01:06:15.430 --> 01:06:20.390
And I feel so lucky to have had John as my scene partner all those years.

1415
01:06:20.610 --> 01:06:22.450
I couldn't have done it with anyone else.

1416
01:06:22.910 --> 01:06:23.770
This I know.

1417
01:06:24.010 --> 01:06:26.930
We shared a brain when it came to Jim and Pam.

1418
01:06:27.230 --> 01:06:27.450
Yeah.

1419
01:06:27.670 --> 01:06:28.130
We did.

1420
01:06:28.330 --> 01:06:29.470
It was really special.

1421
01:06:29.470 --> 01:06:40.710
Yeah, and it is really cool that you're part of this iconic television lore, this amazing couple that so many people love.

1422
01:06:41.010 --> 01:06:41.190
Yeah.

1423
01:06:42.950 --> 01:06:46.570
Well, I'm really glad we finally talked about that.

1424
01:06:48.110 --> 01:06:50.670
Next question is from Rachel N.

1425
01:06:50.810 --> 01:06:51.970
in North Carolina.

1426
01:06:52.350 --> 01:06:55.930
Rachel says, I'm asking this question on behalf of my husband Raj.

1427
01:06:56.290 --> 01:07:03.470
Do you think Pam would have married Roy if Jim had not confessed his love and transferred to Stanford?

1428
01:07:03.470 --> 01:07:05.230
Oh, I love this question.

1429
01:07:05.670 --> 01:07:07.450
No, I don't.

1430
01:07:08.270 --> 01:07:12.150
I don't think Pam would have married Roy.

1431
01:07:12.430 --> 01:07:17.250
I think she would have eventually been honest with herself.

1432
01:07:17.250 --> 01:07:24.970
Like, as the wedding was approaching, I think it's why she wasn't super bummed to have a three-year engagement.

1433
01:07:25.570 --> 01:07:25.830
Yeah.

1434
01:07:26.110 --> 01:07:26.610
You know?

1435
01:07:26.770 --> 01:07:31.670
You know, if you look at Pam and Roy, none of them were trying to push that boulder up the mountain.

1436
01:07:31.990 --> 01:07:39.050
They were just fine to kind of just stay in this sort of purgatory place in their relationship.

1437
01:07:39.410 --> 01:07:42.950
I mean, look at how quickly Pam and Jim get married after they get engaged.

1438
01:07:43.150 --> 01:07:43.450
Yeah.

1439
01:07:43.670 --> 01:07:44.010
You know?

1440
01:07:44.170 --> 01:07:44.470
Yeah.

1441
01:07:44.470 --> 01:07:46.670
So, I don't think she would have.

1442
01:07:46.830 --> 01:07:47.070
No.

1443
01:07:47.290 --> 01:07:48.030
I agree.

1444
01:07:48.510 --> 01:07:50.470
Next question is from Carrie B.

1445
01:07:50.710 --> 01:07:51.750
in Tacoma, Washington.

1446
01:07:52.070 --> 01:07:58.890
Carrie says, Pam's occasionally random friendship with Dwight is one of my favorite character relationships on the show.

1447
01:07:59.090 --> 01:08:03.010
Pam and Dwight were always funny and yet somehow also sincere.

1448
01:08:03.750 --> 01:08:07.150
Jenna, what is your favorite Pam and Dwight moment?

1449
01:08:07.550 --> 01:08:08.190
Oh, my gosh.

1450
01:08:08.270 --> 01:08:08.870
There are so many.

1451
01:08:09.410 --> 01:08:11.510
I mean, I love the injury.

1452
01:08:11.830 --> 01:08:13.030
That's kind of where it starts.

1453
01:08:13.330 --> 01:08:13.470
Yeah.

1454
01:08:13.470 --> 01:08:15.170
I loved vandalism.

1455
01:08:15.850 --> 01:08:22.750
But my favorite storyline with Dwight is Pam's replacement.

1456
01:08:23.430 --> 01:08:33.410
It's where Pam is super pregnant and she realizes that Dwight is the only person who's being honest with her because everyone's telling her how gorgeous she is.

1457
01:08:33.410 --> 01:08:37.110
And then Jim is telling her that he doesn't find Kathy attractive.

1458
01:08:38.090 --> 01:08:43.150
I actually pulled a clip because this scene in particular was one of my favorite to do with rain.

1459
01:08:43.750 --> 01:08:48.270
I know it sounds nuts, but I think Dwight is the only one who's telling me the truth.

1460
01:08:49.230 --> 01:08:51.330
Dwight, am I hot right now?

1461
01:08:51.550 --> 01:08:53.710
Why would I or anyone else think that you're hot right now?

1462
01:08:53.890 --> 01:08:57.450
I can't impregnate you and that's the driving force between male-female attraction.

1463
01:08:57.450 --> 01:08:58.790
What about before?

1464
01:08:58.910 --> 01:08:59.830
Was I attractive before?

1465
01:09:00.490 --> 01:09:00.770
Meh.

1466
01:09:01.530 --> 01:09:07.390
You were at your most attractive when you were 24 with a slight gradual decline and a steep drop-off when you got pregnant for the first time.

1467
01:09:07.610 --> 01:09:12.070
Gradual recovery and, uh, well, now, obviously, you're at an all-time low.

1468
01:09:14.300 --> 01:09:14.580
Hmm.

1469
01:09:15.760 --> 01:09:18.560
I think Jim's lying to me about not being attracted to Kathy.

1470
01:09:19.460 --> 01:09:20.740
You think Jim's lying?

1471
01:09:21.440 --> 01:09:22.400
That's so cute.

1472
01:09:22.820 --> 01:09:24.040
I know he's lying.

1473
01:09:24.340 --> 01:09:26.060
Five bucks if you can get him to admit it.

1474
01:09:26.220 --> 01:09:26.480
Done.

1475
01:09:27.160 --> 01:09:28.540
I never touch a pregnant woman.

1476
01:09:28.820 --> 01:09:30.380
Yep, that's the Dwight I need.

1477
01:09:31.280 --> 01:09:32.100
I love it.

1478
01:09:32.260 --> 01:09:33.520
I love that scene.

1479
01:09:33.760 --> 01:09:37.760
You know, we all have that friend who can really give it to you straight.

1480
01:09:38.000 --> 01:09:38.180
Yeah.

1481
01:09:38.400 --> 01:09:39.880
And it doesn't hurt your feelings.

1482
01:09:40.100 --> 01:09:40.180
Yeah.

1483
01:09:40.180 --> 01:09:43.320
And you actually need it because the rest of the world is gaslighting you.

1484
01:09:43.600 --> 01:09:43.780
Yeah.

1485
01:09:43.780 --> 01:09:45.360
And I love that scene.

1486
01:09:45.540 --> 01:09:50.340
And then, of course, this episode has the whole Mike Tibbetts runner, which was so much fun to shoot.

1487
01:09:50.740 --> 01:09:53.040
So I would have to call that my favorite.

1488
01:09:53.680 --> 01:09:54.140
All right.

1489
01:09:54.240 --> 01:09:56.780
Our last question is from Laura P.

1490
01:09:57.060 --> 01:09:57.760
in Wisconsin.

1491
01:09:58.280 --> 01:10:06.080
Laura says, Jenna, did you ever find it annoying that you are most known slash recognized for playing Pam?

1492
01:10:06.580 --> 01:10:10.120
Do you wish you were recognized for other work?

1493
01:10:10.780 --> 01:10:11.340
No.

1494
01:10:12.140 --> 01:10:15.080
It is my honor to have played Pam.

1495
01:10:15.280 --> 01:10:16.160
I'm getting choked up.

1496
01:10:16.240 --> 01:10:16.920
I don't know why.

1497
01:10:17.660 --> 01:10:18.760
What a legacy.

1498
01:10:19.380 --> 01:10:20.760
I do not need more.

1499
01:10:20.940 --> 01:10:21.620
I don't.

1500
01:10:22.040 --> 01:10:23.220
I don't need more.

1501
01:10:23.220 --> 01:10:29.240
I remember being at Greg Daniels' house at dinner, and the show was coming to an end.

1502
01:10:29.900 --> 01:10:33.220
And I said, I have no more career goals.

1503
01:10:33.820 --> 01:10:34.800
This was it.

1504
01:10:35.020 --> 01:10:41.220
I wanted to play a character that meant something to people on an ensemble television comedy, and I did it.

1505
01:10:41.920 --> 01:10:44.640
That's all I wanted when I came to Los Angeles.

1506
01:10:44.640 --> 01:10:48.940
So it's my honor to be remembered for doing that thing.

1507
01:10:48.940 --> 01:10:51.360
I mean, who gets to do that?

1508
01:10:51.660 --> 01:10:53.020
What a gift.

1509
01:10:53.220 --> 01:10:53.920
What a gift.

1510
01:10:54.400 --> 01:10:56.380
I do want to win a Tony Award, though.

1511
01:10:56.580 --> 01:10:58.000
I do have a new career goal.

1512
01:10:58.020 --> 01:10:58.620
Well, you know what?

1513
01:10:58.680 --> 01:11:00.320
A late in life new career goal.

1514
01:11:00.520 --> 01:11:03.420
We all have goals for our seasons of life.

1515
01:11:03.580 --> 01:11:03.820
Yeah.

1516
01:11:04.120 --> 01:11:06.080
And your goal for this season is a Tony.

1517
01:11:06.720 --> 01:11:09.900
Well, I want to end with this wonderful letter.

1518
01:11:10.480 --> 01:11:14.760
It's going to be our final word here on the All About Pam Beasley episode.

1519
01:11:15.200 --> 01:11:16.780
It's from Allison C.

1520
01:11:16.880 --> 01:11:17.500
in Florida.

1521
01:11:18.180 --> 01:11:20.520
And Allison wrote in and said this.

1522
01:11:20.920 --> 01:11:27.680
When I first watched The Office in middle school, Pam was one of the first characters I ever fully saw myself in.

1523
01:11:27.980 --> 01:11:31.440
My dad describes me as having quiet strength.

1524
01:11:31.860 --> 01:11:35.400
And I don't think there's a better phrase to describe Pam Beasley.

1525
01:11:36.200 --> 01:11:39.740
Watching her blossom as the show went on gave me hope for myself.

1526
01:11:40.060 --> 01:11:42.440
In the final episode, Pam quoted,

1527
01:11:42.880 --> 01:11:48.440
It would just make my heart sore if someone out there saw this and said to herself,

1528
01:11:49.280 --> 01:11:49.880
Be strong.

1529
01:11:50.380 --> 01:11:51.360
Trust yourself.

1530
01:11:51.660 --> 01:11:52.840
Love yourself.

1531
01:11:53.220 --> 01:11:54.560
Conquer your fears.

1532
01:11:54.920 --> 01:11:56.660
Just go after what you want.

1533
01:11:56.900 --> 01:11:58.320
And act faSt.

1534
01:11:58.320 --> 01:12:00.660
Because life just isn't that long.

1535
01:12:01.280 --> 01:12:04.260
Pam would be proud to know that a little girl watching

1536
01:12:04.260 --> 01:12:06.220
latched on to those words.

1537
01:12:06.780 --> 01:12:13.880
I even printed it out and tacked it on my bedroom wall where it remained all throughout my high school years.

1538
01:12:14.480 --> 01:12:21.820
That little girl now has her first office job post-college and loves the office more than ever.

1539
01:12:22.180 --> 01:12:28.300
And that little girl fully believes that Pam was the main subject of the documentary.

1540
01:12:28.300 --> 01:12:30.160
Oh, thank you, Allison.

1541
01:12:30.780 --> 01:12:31.740
She says,

1542
01:12:32.140 --> 01:12:34.080
That's my personal soapbox.

1543
01:12:34.420 --> 01:12:38.100
From the quiet, creative girls all around the world,

1544
01:12:38.360 --> 01:12:43.200
thank you, Jenna, for showing us that there's a lot of beauty in ordinary things.

1545
01:12:43.400 --> 01:12:46.260
That is kind of the point after all.

1546
01:12:46.800 --> 01:12:47.400
Wow.

1547
01:12:47.400 --> 01:12:48.600
Oh, my gosh.

1548
01:12:48.920 --> 01:12:51.880
That really moved me.

1549
01:12:54.150 --> 01:12:55.970
You know, that quote, I said it.

1550
01:12:56.030 --> 01:12:56.790
I didn't write it.

1551
01:12:57.170 --> 01:12:59.790
That is the heart of Greg Daniels.

1552
01:13:01.290 --> 01:13:11.990
That's the message he wanted people to take from this character and this beautiful arc that he crafted along with all the other writers for me to play.

1553
01:13:11.990 --> 01:13:18.170
And, I mean, letters like that are exactly why I don't need to be remembered for doing anything else.

1554
01:13:18.750 --> 01:13:20.210
It's just beautiful.

1555
01:13:20.530 --> 01:13:20.910
Yeah.

1556
01:13:21.090 --> 01:13:21.930
It really is.

1557
01:13:21.990 --> 01:13:22.390
It is.

1558
01:13:22.630 --> 01:13:24.910
Well, we just love you guys so much.

1559
01:13:25.070 --> 01:13:34.550
Thank you so much for listening to Office Ladies and Office Ladies 6.0 and your support over the years and just these great questions.

1560
01:13:34.690 --> 01:13:36.750
We just love doing this.

1561
01:13:36.810 --> 01:13:37.790
And thank you.

1562
01:13:37.910 --> 01:13:38.970
Thank you so much.

1563
01:13:38.970 --> 01:13:42.790
And thank you, Sam, Cassie, and Angela, for my birthday celebration.

1564
01:13:43.250 --> 01:13:51.470
You know, last year I turned 50, and I didn't get a big birthday because I was actually in chemotherapy for my 50th birthday.

1565
01:13:51.770 --> 01:13:57.010
So this year is an extra special milestone, and you've kicked it off in the best way.

1566
01:13:57.170 --> 01:13:57.490
Thank you.

1567
01:13:57.690 --> 01:13:59.090
Well, let's go eat some cake.

1568
01:13:59.210 --> 01:13:59.970
Let's do it.

1569
01:14:00.150 --> 01:14:01.570
You guys have a great week.

1570
